WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.367 --> 00:00:11.551
<v Daniel>Imagine graduating in the top 10% of your class with a computer science degree. For the last 20 years, that has been the absolute safest bet in the modern economy.</v>

2
00:00:11.671 --> 00:00:12.471
<v Maya>Oh, absolutely.</v>

3
00:00:13.391 --> 00:00:21.574
<v Daniel>You walk across the stage, you grab your diploma, and you step out into a labor market where your starting salary has effectively traveled back in time a full decade.</v>

4
00:00:22.217 --> 00:00:31.419
<v Maya>Yeah, I mean, it is a staggering reversal of fortunes. We are literally watching the foundation of the knowledge economy shift in real time.</v>

5
00:00:31.699 --> 00:00:46.501
<v Daniel>Right. And if you are listening to this, you are probably trying to figure out if that shift is a temporary tremor or a permanent fracture. You want the hard evidence about what is actually happening to the newest generation of college grads entering an AI-saturated</v>

6
00:00:46.541 --> 00:00:47.101
<v Daniel>labor market.</v>

7
00:00:47.301 --> 00:00:47.761
<v Maya>Exactly.</v>

8
00:00:48.001 --> 00:01:01.114
<v Daniel>Like, is the first rung of the career ladder just gone? So welcome to this deep dive into a stack of fresh working papers. Today, our mission centers on one incredibly explosive new working paper from September 2026.</v>

9
00:01:01.415 --> 00:01:03.015
<v Maya>Fresh out of the U.S. Census Bureau.</v>

10
00:01:03.135 --> 00:01:16.582
<v Daniel>Right. It's called Graduating into Disruption by Cody Orr, Lee Tucker and Lawrence Warren. And joining me to help translate this massive economic analysis into clear insights is our resident expert on all things labor and data.</v>

11
00:01:17.128 --> 00:01:27.396
<v Maya>It is a privilege to be diving into this one with you because to really grasp the magnitude of the shock happening here, we first, we have to appreciate how these researchers actually managed to isolate it.</v>

12
00:01:27.636 --> 00:01:29.617
<v Daniel>Yeah, without falling into the usual data traps.</v>

13
00:01:29.677 --> 00:01:29.878
<v Maya>Right.</v>

14
00:01:30.698 --> 00:01:41.086
<v Daniel>Labor economics is usually plagued by, you know, tiny sample sizes or self-reported surveys. But the data engine behind this ore paper is virtually unprecedented in its scale.</v>

15
00:01:41.284 --> 00:01:48.366
<v Maya>They aren't just calling up alumni and asking how the job hunt is going. They linked two massive administrative data sets together.</v>

16
00:01:48.526 --> 00:02:02.571
<v Daniel>Huge ones. On one side, you have the post-secondary employment outcomes data set, which is basically the official graduation records covering about 29% of all U.S. bachelor's degrees from 2016 to 2024.</v>

17
00:02:03.831 --> 00:02:06.412
<v Daniel>And then they link that to the LEHD data.</v>

18
00:02:07.045 --> 00:02:21.975
<v Maya>Which is such a vital piece of the puzzle. LEHD stands for Longitudinal Employer Household Dynamics, which simply put is State Unemployment Insurance Wage Records. It's the raw tax data that tracks exactly what a person is getting paid and the exact industry where</v>

19
00:02:21.995 --> 00:02:22.315
<v Maya>they work.</v>

20
00:02:22.575 --> 00:02:23.856
<v Daniel>So there's no hiding.</v>

21
00:02:24.576 --> 00:02:30.060
<v Maya>You can't claim on LinkedIn that you're a freelance tech consultant when your W-2 says you're working at a coffee shop.</v>

22
00:02:30.240 --> 00:02:30.680
<v Daniel>Exactly.</v>

23
00:02:31.160 --> 00:02:31.941
<v Maya>The data doesn't lie.</v>

24
00:02:32.320 --> 00:02:41.827
<v Daniel>And just as a quick fact for you listening, to understand the scale here, the City University of New York, CUNY, is actually one of the massive data partners making this linkage possible.</v>

25
00:02:42.547 --> 00:02:43.228
<v Maya>Oh, wow. Yeah.</v>

26
00:02:43.628 --> 00:02:57.678
<v Daniel>We're talking about tracking over 6.6 million bachelor's graduates across 24 states. But having all that data is really only half the battle. The super clever part is how they actually measure AI exposure.</v>

27
00:02:58.300 --> 00:03:06.985
<v Maya>Right. That design choice is the true genius of the paper. Because if you only look at graduates who successfully get hired as software engineers and you say, look, they're exposed to AI.</v>

28
00:03:07.145 --> 00:03:09.246
<v Daniel>Yeah, you're totally missing the people who got locked out.</v>

29
00:03:09.606 --> 00:03:22.994
<v Maya>Exactly. You're completely missing the grads who never even got their foot in the door. So they scored AI exposure by college major. They looked at the typical occupations that young grads of those specific majors held before ChatGPT came out.</v>

30
00:03:23.174 --> 00:03:24.075
<v Daniel>Ah, so pre-2022.</v>

31
00:03:24.835 --> 00:03:31.279
<v Maya>Yes. They map those historical jobs to GPT-4 task exposure scores to establish a really rigid</v>

32
00:03:31.319 --> 00:03:44.368
<v Daniel>baseline. It's like judging a swimmer by the lane they were assigned, not their finish time. Because the major is locked in long before the job search even begins, so it doesn't condition on whether they actually found a job in their field.</v>

33
00:03:44.694 --> 00:03:55.005
<v Maya>That is a perfect way to visualize it. And when you structure the data that way, isolating the intended path from the outcome, the findings just, well, they hit you like a freight train.</v>

34
00:03:55.825 --> 00:03:59.129
<v Daniel>Okay. Let's get into the numbers then. Give us the bad news.</v>

35
00:03:59.813 --> 00:04:14.621
<v Maya>All right. For those needing clear, undeniable stats, here they are. The top decile of AI-exposed majors, which is heavily dominated by computer science, IT, computer engineering, they saw a five percentage point drop in employment the</v>

36
00:04:14.681 --> 00:04:16.862
<v Maya>quarter immediately following graduation.</v>

37
00:04:17.802 --> 00:04:19.303
<v Daniel>A five point drop right out of the gate.</v>

38
00:04:19.814 --> 00:04:33.810
<v Maya>Yes. And a 13% drop in initial full quarter earnings. Wow. It gets worse. By 2025, the initial earnings for that top decile were down 14 log points compared to the least exposed majors.</v>

39
00:04:34.151 --> 00:04:35.512
<v Daniel>So roughly a 14% decline?</v>

40
00:04:36.921 --> 00:04:51.894
<v Maya>A 13%, a 14% drop in earnings right out of school is massive. But that raises an immediate question about the mechanics of that drop. Like, where is that coming from? Are tech companies just looking at these new grads and saying, hey, we have AI to help</v>

41
00:04:51.934 --> 00:04:57.318
<v Maya>you. Your job is easier, so we're paying you 13% less? Or is something more structural happening?</v>

42
00:04:57.779 --> 00:05:03.624
<v Daniel>What's fascinating here is that about half of this entire earnings decline is sectoral.</v>

43
00:05:04.564 --> 00:05:05.085
<v Maya>Sectoral.</v>

44
00:05:05.512 --> 00:05:07.173
<v Daniel>Meaning they're in completely different industries.</v>

45
00:05:07.313 --> 00:05:16.640
<v Maya>Exactly. It's a composition effect. Those top decile graduates became more than two percentage points more likely to work in accommodation, food services, and retail</v>

46
00:05:16.680 --> 00:05:22.044
<v Daniel>trade. So the grad who spent four years grinding through data structures is now like pouring coffee.</v>

47
00:05:22.525 --> 00:05:37.294
<v Maya>Or managing inventory at a clothing store. Because the tech sector gate is locked. Meanwhile, the core sectors that traditionally absorb them, professional, scientific, and technical services, lost nearly six percentage points of their employment share. Wow. And information lost</v>

48
00:05:37.374 --> 00:05:52.178
<v Maya>over three points. Without the shift in retail and hospitality, their earnings would still have fallen nearly 8% due to lower demand. But with that shift, earnings fell 15%. That effectively wipes out a decade of wage growth,</v>

49
00:05:52.358 --> 00:05:54.958
<v Maya>returning starting salaries to pre-2016 levels.</v>

50
00:05:55.378 --> 00:06:07.401
<v Daniel>Okay, let's unpack this because a 13% to 15% drop sounds exactly like what happens when a generation graduates into a total economic collapse. How does this compare to, say, graduating into a massive recession?</v>

51
00:06:07.693 --> 00:06:18.137
<v Maya>The authors actually tackle that exact comparison. They benchmark against a really well-known 2012 paper by Philip Oriopoulos, which looked at Canadian graduates entering the market during severe recessions.</v>

52
00:06:18.538 --> 00:06:19.258
<v Daniel>Man, what did that show?</v>

53
00:06:19.832 --> 00:06:25.057
<v Maya>A typical large recession yields an initial earnings loss of about 9% to 10%.</v>

54
00:06:25.177 --> 00:06:30.481
<v Daniel>Wait, really? So the AI shock of 13% is actually larger than graduating into a recession?</v>

55
00:06:30.622 --> 00:06:31.843
<v Maya>It is larger, yes.</v>

56
00:06:31.983 --> 00:06:32.183
<v Daniel>Wow.</v>

57
00:06:32.363 --> 00:06:40.070
<v Maya>The initial impact is larger. Now, the authors caveat that the measurement methodologies differ slightly, but the sheer magnitude is undeniable.</v>

58
00:06:40.410 --> 00:06:41.031
<v Daniel>That's terrifying.</v>

59
00:06:41.409 --> 00:06:50.595
<v Maya>But the real tension here isn't just the initial shock. It's the scarring. Oriopoulos found that less-advantaged grads lose about 8% of their cumulative earnings over the next 10 years.</v>

60
00:06:50.735 --> 00:06:53.497
<v Daniel>Because they get stuck at lower-paying employers and just can't climb out.</v>

61
00:06:53.717 --> 00:06:54.197
<v Maya>Exactly.</v>

62
00:06:54.217 --> 00:06:54.657
<v Daniel>Right.</v>

63
00:06:54.937 --> 00:06:59.580
<v Maya>So the question is, do the AI-exposed grads catch up, or are they scarred?</v>

64
00:06:59.680 --> 00:07:03.883
<v Daniel>Right. Do we see that in the OR data? Are the tech grads bouncing back after a year or two?</v>

65
00:07:04.163 --> 00:07:12.732
<v Maya>We're seeing a very worrying divergence. They sell seven to nine and largely recover within one to two years. But the absolute top decile does not.</v>

66
00:07:12.792 --> 00:07:13.372
<v Daniel>They're still stuck.</v>

67
00:07:13.592 --> 00:07:24.276
<v Maya>Yeah. At two years out, they still have about two percentage points lower employment. And at one year out, their earnings are still trailing by 8.1 log points, so roughly 8%.</v>

68
00:07:24.916 --> 00:07:33.159
<v Daniel>But, I mean, I have to play the skeptic here. Anytime we look at economic data from late 2022 to the present, there are so many overlapping crises.</v>

69
00:07:33.839 --> 00:07:34.419
<v Maya>Absolutely.</v>

70
00:07:34.909 --> 00:07:46.454
<v Daniel>How can they be so sure this is an AI story? Like, what about basic oversupply? The whole world spent a decade telling millennials and Gen Z to learn to code. Couldn't the market just be flooded with computer science majors?</v>

71
00:07:47.062 --> 00:07:58.511
<v Maya>It's a very logical counter-argument, and the authors do test it directly. The number of top DeSalle graduates did grow by a massive 24% from 2017 to 2022.</v>

72
00:07:58.611 --> 00:08:00.113
<v Daniel>Which is a huge flood of supply.</v>

73
00:08:00.353 --> 00:08:09.860
<v Maya>True, but here's the catch. Graduate employment kept pace perfectly with that surge right up until the exact moment Chad GPT was released.</v>

74
00:08:09.880 --> 00:08:10.161
<v Daniel>Oh, wow.</v>

75
00:08:10.901 --> 00:08:19.510
<v Maya>Only then did the number of employed graduates suddenly fall by 3%. And that happened even as the growth in new graduates was actually starting to slow down.</v>

76
00:08:19.951 --> 00:08:26.738
<v Daniel>Okay. But what about grads just, you know, deciding to wait it out, going back to grad school or starting freelance gigs?</v>

77
00:08:27.303 --> 00:08:35.088
<v Maya>There was a slight uptick there. Top decile grad school enrollment rose from 16.2 percent to 17.86 percent.</v>

78
00:08:35.348 --> 00:08:36.129
<v Daniel>OK, so a bump.</v>

79
00:08:36.469 --> 00:08:50.077
<v Maya>Yeah. And the authors acknowledge that grad school and self-employment could theoretically explain up to half of that five point employment drop. But they read those trends as clear signs of a weak labor market, not people pursuing exciting new opportunities.</v>

80
00:08:50.718 --> 00:09:02.161
<v Daniel>Fair enough. But there is a much bigger elephant in the room. Monetary tightening. In 2022, the Fed hiked interest rates aggressively. Tech valuations crashed. Layoffs happened.</v>

81
00:09:02.582 --> 00:09:03.682
<v Maya>Right. The end of cheap money.</v>

82
00:09:04.023 --> 00:09:04.443
<v Daniel>Exactly.</v>

83
00:09:05.204 --> 00:09:14.650
<v Maya>To isolate that, the Orr paper uses a 2026 companion paper by Lee Tucker. Tucker looked at the industry-level evidence across 10 quarters post-chat GPT.</v>

84
00:09:15.011 --> 00:09:15.671
<v Daniel>And what did he find?</v>

85
00:09:16.302 --> 00:09:26.466
<v Maya>He found that employment of 22 to 24 year olds in the most exposed sectors fell by 12 percent. And when he mathematically modeled the impact of monetary policy</v>

86
00:09:26.826 --> 00:09:27.946
<v Daniel>it couldn't explain all of it.</v>

87
00:09:28.206 --> 00:09:38.970
<v Maya>Not even close. It explains at most about a quarter of that decline. The drop is just too steep and too focused on specific exposed tasks to be purely a product of interest rates.</v>

88
00:09:39.443 --> 00:09:47.350
<v Daniel>Okay, but here's where it gets really interesting. The strongest objection I've seen is this fascinating 2026 paper by Lambert and Schindler.</v>

89
00:09:47.831 --> 00:09:49.532
<v Maya>Ah, yes, the work-from-home objection.</v>

90
00:09:49.953 --> 00:09:58.800
<v Daniel>Right. They looked at a staggering 243 million new hires. And they argue this might not be an AI story at all. It might be a remote work story.</v>

91
00:09:59.381 --> 00:10:01.263
<v Maya>This is the collinearity problem.</v>

92
00:10:01.603 --> 00:10:04.766
<v Daniel>It's probably the most fiercely debated tension in labor economics right now.</v>

93
00:10:05.147 --> 00:10:06.588
<v Maya>Let's define that for the listeners.</v>

94
00:10:06.888 --> 00:10:19.295
<v Daniel>A collinearity problem is, well, it's basically when two suspect vehicles are driving side by side down the highway at the exact same speed. When a crash happens, you mathematically can't tell which car caused it.</v>

95
00:10:20.173 --> 00:10:35.098
<v Maya>That is a perfect analogy. And here, the two vehicles are AI exposure and work from home exposure. Lambert and Schindler point out that the jobs most exposed to AI have a massive 0.88 correlation by college major with</v>

96
00:10:35.118 --> 00:10:36.899
<v Maya>the jobs that are easiest to do from home.</v>

97
00:10:37.519 --> 00:10:44.141
<v Daniel>So sitting at a computer writing code can be done by a chat bot, but it can also be done by a human in their pajamas in another time zone.</v>

98
00:10:44.521 --> 00:10:45.021
<v Maya>Exactly.</v>

99
00:10:45.802 --> 00:10:48.062
<v Daniel>So how do Lambert and Schindler separate those two vehicles?</v>

100
00:10:48.783 --> 00:10:49.843
<v Maya>They estimate them together.</v>

101
00:10:50.332 --> 00:11:05.277
<v Daniel>Their findings are super provocative. When tested side by side, the remote work effect survives robustly, predicting about a 5% fall in the junior share of hires. But the AI effect often drops sharply, sometimes going all the way to zero, because</v>

102
00:11:05.457 --> 00:11:12.599
<v Daniel>remote work raises the cost of training a junior employee so much. I mean, onboarding a 22-year-old over Zoom is exhausting.</v>

103
00:11:12.940 --> 00:11:13.240
<v Maya>It is.</v>

104
00:11:13.780 --> 00:11:20.102
<v Daniel>You lose all that overheard-in-the-hallway osmosis. So managers just hold out for a senior developer who already knows what they're doing.</v>

105
00:11:20.603 --> 00:11:22.424
<v Maya>That's the Lambert and Schindler thesis.</v>

106
00:11:22.964 --> 00:11:28.747
<v Daniel>But to balance the scales impartially, the OR team did run checks for remote work in their specific graduate data set.</v>

107
00:11:28.987 --> 00:11:29.528
<v Maya>And what happened?</v>

108
00:11:29.928 --> 00:11:44.856
<v Daniel>They found it barely moved their AI estimates. So neither paper fully resolves the collinearity. It's a genuine tension. We're likely looking at a compounding disaster for new grads. Work from home made juniors harder to train, and</v>

109
00:11:44.936 --> 00:11:47.297
<v Daniel>AI made their entry-level tasks easier to automate.</v>

110
00:11:47.693 --> 00:11:59.904
<v Maya>Okay, but if we assume the AI effect is real and majorly driving this, there's a timeline anomaly that bothers me. Why are new grads suffering while older, established tech workers seem totally fine?</v>

111
00:12:00.104 --> 00:12:04.047
<v Daniel>Right, because the broader unemployment rate for senior engineers hasn't skyrocketed.</v>

112
00:12:04.408 --> 00:12:14.737
<v Maya>And that perfectly reconciles a split in the literature. Orrin Tucker pointed out that studies of young workers find huge declines, but studies of people already employed find very little displacement.</v>

113
00:12:15.045 --> 00:12:16.566
<v Daniel>So the damage is entirely at the gate.</v>

114
00:12:16.906 --> 00:12:23.348
<v Maya>Exactly. It shows up as delayed employment and worse first jobs, which national unemployment rates completely miss.</v>

115
00:12:23.868 --> 00:12:36.512
<v Daniel>But why did companies lock the gate so early? The hiring freeze for new grads happened in late 2022 and early 2023. Most firms hadn't even adopted enterprise AI yet. They were still arguing about data privacy.</v>

116
00:12:37.073 --> 00:12:37.273
<v Maya>Right.</v>

117
00:12:37.673 --> 00:12:43.795
<v Daniel>So if they weren't replacing juniors with AI yet, why did they stop hiring them? Is it just like a trust issue?</v>

118
00:12:44.410 --> 00:12:55.313
<v Maya>If we connect this to the bigger picture, you've hit on the most insidious mechanism here. To understand this trust collapse, we have to look at a 2026 paper by Igor Chirikov from UC Berkeley.</v>

119
00:12:55.813 --> 00:12:57.733
<v Daniel>Let me guess. Grade inflation.</v>

120
00:12:58.113 --> 00:13:12.997
<v Maya>Extreme grade inflation. He analyzed over 500,000 grades. And he found that in AI-exposed courses, like writing and coding, the share of A grades rose by 13 percentage points immediately after ChatGPT. That's insane.</v>

121
00:13:13.652 --> 00:13:20.456
<v Maya>That represents a 30% jump over the 2022 baseline. And it was even higher in classes where homework counted more.</v>

122
00:13:20.817 --> 00:13:35.006
<v Daniel>So this is the signaling channel. Employers rely on transcripts to send a signal of confidence. But if every resume has a 4.0 GPA, the hiring manager has no idea if the candidate's a brilliant coder or just really good at prompting an AI to do their homework.</v>

123
00:13:35.603 --> 00:13:47.966
<v Maya>The signal is broken. The traditional take home coding tests and remote work samples became instantly obsolete. Employers froze entry level hiring because they knew the students had A.I. and they couldn't verify real skills anymore.</v>

124
00:13:48.446 --> 00:13:57.828
<v Daniel>So what does this all mean for higher education? What is the playbook? Do universities look at this 15 percent earnings drop and tell students to stop majoring in computer science?</v>

125
00:13:58.498 --> 00:14:08.183
<v Maya>The prescription from the data is absolutely not to abandon computing. We will still desperately need engineers. The imperative is to change what is taught, and more importantly, how it's assessed.</v>

126
00:14:08.763 --> 00:14:10.004
<v Daniel>How do they rebuild that trust?</v>

127
00:14:10.584 --> 00:14:20.109
<v Maya>They have to pivot to credentialing real skills, moving away from take-home assignments to supervised assessments, verified portfolios, and heavily supervised work-integrated learning.</v>

128
00:14:20.670 --> 00:14:24.772
<v Daniel>And I imagine employers have to step up too. They can't just wait for perfect candidates.</v>

129
00:14:25.090 --> 00:14:31.734
<v Maya>Absolutely. Universities need to partner directly with employers to subsidize or completely rebuild the training rung of entry-level jobs.</v>

130
00:14:32.434 --> 00:14:39.739
<v Daniel>Because while we wait for these solutions, we have to acknowledge the human cost. When the first rung of the ladder breaks, who is most exposed?</v>

131
00:14:40.559 --> 00:14:48.984
<v Maya>Drawing an inference back to the Oreopolis recession data, the people most exposed are early career workers, adult learners, and first-generation students.</v>

132
00:14:49.673 --> 00:14:55.515
<v Daniel>Right. The people who don't have an uncle at the firm. They don't have the family networks to bypass a broken hiring process.</v>

133
00:14:55.675 --> 00:15:04.399
<v Maya>Yes. They lack the financial buffer for a long job search. So they take the retail or food service job to pay the bills and they suffer that long term scarring.</v>

134
00:15:05.139 --> 00:15:19.589
<v Daniel>It's a sobering reality check. Let's just quickly summarize the journey here. We went from a 13 percent earnings shock driven by sectoral downgrading. through this tangled web of remote work and great inflation, to the absolute imperative for</v>

135
00:15:19.649 --> 00:15:20.770
<v Daniel>institutional reform.</v>

136
00:15:21.471 --> 00:15:23.172
<v Maya>And this raises an important question.</v>

137
00:15:24.053 --> 00:15:36.286
<v Daniel>If entry-level jobs have historically served as the subsidized training ground for tomorrow's experts, and AI automates that ground away, how exactly does society plan to create the senior experts of the year 2035?</v>

138
00:15:37.602 --> 00:15:50.974
<v Maya>That is the multi-trillion dollar question hanging over everything right now. Thank you for joining us on this deep dive into the source material. We respect your time and we encourage you to keep exploring the evidence shaping the future of work. Until next time.</v>
